+		    separate graphs, you can mark each part as a <i>region</i> and fit a separate model to each one.</p>
+		<p><b>Making a Region - </b>Click <b>+ Region</b> (below the fit results), then drag across the graph from where the
+		    part starts to where it ends.  A shaded band marks the region, and it gets a number (regions are numbered
+		    left to right).  Now choose a model from the list, just as you would for the whole sheet.  nPlot fits only the
+		    points inside the band, draws the curve only across the band, and shows that region's <i>A</i>, <i>B</i>,
+		    <i>C</i>, and RMSE.  Repeat for each part of the graph.</p>
+		<p><b>Switching Between Regions - </b>Tap a region's number in the row of buttons, or tap inside its band on the
+		    graph.  The selected region's band is darker, its points are circled, and the model list and results show
+		    that region.  <b>All points</b> goes back to the model for the whole sheet, which works exactly as before.</p>
+		<p><b>Changing or Deleting a Region - </b>Drag the handle on either edge of the selected region to move that edge.
+		    Tap the &times; on the selected region's button to delete it.  An edge dropped close to a neighboring region's
+		    edge, or to the first or last data point, snaps to it, so neighboring regions share their boundary.  A point exactly on a shared boundary belongs to
+		    both regions.  Regions belong to one sheet; each sheet can have its own.  Clearing a sheet, swapping
+		    <i>x</i> and <i>y</i>, or linearizing removes the regions, because their <i>x</i> ranges no longer apply.</p>
+		<p><b>What Works with Regions - </b>All the Basic and Advanced models.  Expert models need their starting guesses
+		    and only work on a whole sheet.  The Tangent Finder works on the selected region.  <b>Copy Slopes</b> copies
+		    the selected region's slopes, or, with All points selected, the slope at every point using the fit of the
+		    region it is in, which turns a piecewise position graph into one velocity table.  <b>Add Fit</b> in the
+		    Annotate dialog lists each region's results.  Save to File keeps the regions; Save Graph (in the browser)
